fss-adsb (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * dump1090: fix the altitude clamp on 32-bit targets. UINT32_MAX does not
    fit in a long on armhf, so the clamp bound was -1 and every clamped
    altitude wrapped to 0xFFFFFFFF.
  * Measure every duration on CLOCK_MONOTONIC rather than gettimeofday():
    connect deadlines, reconnect backoff, per-field freshness, aircraft
    eviction and TSLC. On the RTC-less SBCs these receivers run on, a routine
    forward NTP step used to expire every accumulated field and evict every
    aircraft at once. The wire timestamp is reconstructed at send time, so no
    monotonic value leaks into an FSS message.
  * dump1090: enable TCP keepalive (120s idle + 20s * 3), bounding detection
    of a peer that died without RST/FIN to roughly 3 minutes instead of the
    kernel default of about 2h11m. The receive thread previously stayed
    blocked in recv() indefinitely, indistinguishable from empty airspace.
  * dump1090: drop messages whose ICAO address is empty, non-hex, zero or
    wider than 24 bits, instead of folding them into one phantom aircraft
    0x000000 that was reported to FSS as a real contact.
  * dump1090: reject unparseable, non-finite or out-of-range coordinates
    rather than reporting the aircraft at Null Island; a garbage position no
    longer discards a usable altitude from the same message.
  * dump1090: parse altitude, vertical rate and squawk strictly, so garbage
    such as "1200x" is rejected rather than becoming a valid-looking 0.
    Syntactically valid out-of-range values still clamp where a clamped value
    is plausible.
  * dump1090: reject semantically impossible squawk codes (above 7777, or
    containing a decimal digit 8 or 9) instead of clamping them into a
    plausible-looking value.
  * adsb: reject over-length callsigns rather than storing them. The SBS-1
    callsign field is 8 characters, but line framing allowed a field of
    several kilobytes to be accepted and retained per aircraft; it was the
    only field on the wire with no validation at all.
  * adsb: bound the aircraft registry at 4096 tracked aircraft, evicting the
    least-recently-heard when full. It previously grew without limit between
    the 10-minute eviction sweeps, so a feed emitting distinct addresses
    could exhaust memory on the armhf targets this package ships to. Reaching
    capacity is logged.
  * adsb: trim SBS-1's trailing space padding from callsigns, which broke
    exact-string consumers downstream.
  * adsb: expire stale per-field telemetry, so a heading or speed observed
    minutes ago no longer rides along on every later position report looking
    as current as one seen just now.
  * dump1090: preserve the fractional ground speed and track the wire
    carries, and range-check track to [0, 360].
  * dump1090: use the altitude carried by MSG,5, MSG,6 and MSG,7, so Mode-S
    targets without ADS-B Out gain an altitude. MSG,2 (surface position) is
    handled explicitly: it reports nothing but keeps last_seen fresh.
  * dump1090: try every getaddrinfo result rather than only the first, so a
    dual-stack host whose preferred family is unroutable no longer fails
    forever. Resolution happens fresh on every attempt.
  * units: round feet/minute to cm/s to nearest, matching the other two
    conversions.
  * main: validate the TLS credential paths at startup and exit non-zero if
    any is unreadable. An unreadable key previously left the process running
    indefinitely, logging the same error every few seconds, with systemd
    reporting the unit as healthy and Restart=on-failure never firing.
  * Decouple dump1090 ingestion from FSS reporting: reporting runs on its own
    thread fed by a bounded, ICAO-coalescing queue whose push() never blocks,
    so a stalled FSS peer no longer stalls the next recv(). Positions are
    reported with their receive time rather than their send time.
  * reporting_worker: bound the shutdown drain to 5s, so a stalled FSS peer
    no longer hangs `systemctl stop` until SIGKILL at TimeoutStopSec.
  * Run as a dedicated unprivileged fss-adsb user (UID/GID 963, pinned
    identically in debian/postinst and docker/Dockerfile) under a full
    systemd sandbox, rather than as root: fss-adsb parses attacker-reachable
    RF-derived data while holding FSS client TLS private keys.
  * debian: ship a templated systemd unit, fss-adsb@.service. The instance
    name selects an environment file /etc/fss-adsb/<instance>.conf, matching
    the one-instance-per-FSS-server deployment model; instances are not
    enabled by default. An example configuration is installed under
    /usr/share/doc/fss-adsb/examples/.
  * debian: add an extended Description (lintian
    extended-description-is-empty).
  * main: add --help/-h and --version.
  * docker: scope the Canterbury Air Patrol apt signing key with signed-by
    instead of installing it into trusted.gpg.d, where it was an acceptable
    signer for every configured repository including the distribution's own.
  * docker: multi-stage build, so the runtime image carries no toolchain;
    fail fast on a missing environment variable.
  * Require flight-safety-system >= 1.3.0, enforced by both configure.ac and
    the packaging. 1.3.0 moved all four libraries .so.3 -> .so.4, so this
    package has to be rebuilt against it; mixing the two ABIs corrupts object
    layout at runtime rather than failing to link. It subsumes the earlier
    >= 1.0.2 floor, which existed for the internally synchronised connection
    pointer reportAircraft() relies on.
  * build: support Catch2 v3 via pkg-config, falling back to the vendored v2
    header where no v3 package exists (Debian bookworm).
  * ci: publish Docker images for bookworm as well as trixie and resolute;
    exercise the Catch2 v2 fallback branch, which no job previously reached;
    and cross-check AC_INIT against debian/changelog.
  * check-code.sh: actually fail on clang-tidy warnings. `set -e` exempts a
    command inverted with `!`, so the gate had been reporting findings and
    exiting 0 since the version cross-checks were appended after it.

 -- Scott Parlane <sjp@canterburyairpatrol.org>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

fss-adsb (1.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * dump1090: close the socket when the receive thread observes a dropped
    connection, fixing a file-descriptor leak per drop/reconnect cycle.
  * dump1090: take the ADS-B callback as a constructor parameter so it is in
    place before the receive thread starts (closes a registration race).
  * dump1090: guard empty groundspeed/track/vertrate (MSG,4) and squawk
    (MSG,6) fields so a missing field is not reported as a valid zero value;
    clamp ground speed and squawk parsing like the other numeric fields.
  * dump1090: handle MSG,2 (surface position) explicitly; it reports nothing
    but keeps the aircraft's last_seen fresh.
  * dump1090: cap the reconnect backoff exactly at 30s; pass getaddrinfo()
    hints (SOCK_STREAM, AI_ADDRCONFIG) when resolving the dump1090 host.
  * main: send the position report outside the aircraft-map lock so a stalled
    TLS send cannot block stale-aircraft eviction.
  * fss-reporter: drop client_lock; rely on flight-safety-system's internally
    synchronised connection pointer so a blocking reconnect no longer stalls
    reportAircraft() on the dump1090 receive thread.
  * Bump version to 1.0.0; drop unused libtool/pkg-config-install/
    pthread-check machinery and undefined Makefile.am variables.
  * README: the example now points at dump1090's SBS-1 port 30003 (not the
    raw AVR port 30002, which fss-adsb cannot parse).
  * debian: modernise packaging style
    - Switch from debian/compat to debhelper-compat (= 13) in Build-Depends
    - Update Standards-Version to 4.7.0
    - Add ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends} to binary package Depends
    - Fix debian/copyright placeholders
    - Drop bogus dh_auto_configure override
  * debian: Section utils; drop unused systemd and autotools-dev
    Build-Depends; depend on every FSS library actually linked (libfss,
    libfss-transport, libfss-transport-ssl, libfss-client-ssl), all
    versioned >= 1.0.2.
  * ci: valgrind now runs with --track-fds=yes to catch fd leaks.

 -- Scott Parlane <sjp@canterburyairpatrol.org>  Fri, 12 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000

